(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["interpretation-of-medical-literature-book-questions-chapter-06-risk-exposure-to-disease-quiz"]={"title":"Chapter 6: Risk: Exposure to Disease Quiz — Book Questions — Interpretation of Medical Literature","category":"Interpretation of Medical Literature","exam":"Book Questions","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which of the following statements is not correct for both prospective and retrospective cohort studies?","choices":["They measure incidence of disease directly.","They allow assessment of possible associations between exposure and many diseases.","They allow investigators to decide beforehand what data to collect.","They avoid bias that might occur if measurement of exposure is made after the outcome of interest is known."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, this is the statement that is NOT true of both. Only a prospective cohort lets investigators decide beforehand what to collect; a retrospective (historical) cohort must use records made earlier for other purposes, so it cannot examine characteristics that were never recorded and exposure may not have been measured in a standardized way. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 12; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 14; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 16; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.1"},{"q":"A study was done examining the relationship of smoking, stroke, and age. The 12-year incidence per 1,000 persons (absolute risk) of stroke according to age and smoking status was:\n\n45–49: Nonsmokers 7.4, Smokers 29.7; 65–69: Nonsmokers 80.2, Smokers 110.4.\n\nWhich of the following statements about the study results is incorrect?","choices":["To calculate population-attributable risk of smoking among people in their 60s, additional data are needed.","More cases of stroke due to smoking occurred in people in their 60s than in their 40s.","When relative risk is calculated, the results reflect information about the incidence in exposed and unexposed persons, whereas the results for attributable risk do not.","The calculated relative risk is a stronger argument for smoking as cause of stroke for persons in their 40s than the calculated risk for persons in their 60s.","Depending on the question asked, age could be considered as either a confounding variable or an effect modifier in the study."],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct, this statement is the incorrect one. Both measures are built from the incidence in exposed and in unexposed persons: relative risk is their ratio and attributable risk is their difference. What differs is that the ratio hides how large the absolute incidence is, but it still uses both incidences. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 18; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 26; 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 28; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.4"},{"q":"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that occasionally can lead to pulmonary embolism and death. The incidence of DVT is increased by several genetic and environmental factors, including oral contraceptives (OCs) and a genetic mutation, factor V Leiden. These two risk factors, OCs and factor V Leiden, interact. Heterozygotes for factor V Leiden have 4 to 10 times the risk of DVT of the general population. In women without the genetic mutation, incidence of DVT rises from about 0.8/10,000 women/yr among those not on OCs to 3.0/10,000 women/yr for those taking the pill. The baseline incidence of DVT in heterozygotes for factor V Leiden is 5.7/10,000 women/yr, rising to 28.5/10,000 women/yr among those taking OCs. Mutations for factor V Leiden occur in about 5% of whites but are absent in Africans and Asians.\n\nWhat is the absolute risk of DVT in women who do not have the mutation and do not take OCs?","choices":["0.8/10,000/yr","1.3/10,000/yr","2.2/10,000/yr","9.5/10,000/yr","25.5/10,000/yr"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: absolute risk is another term for incidence, the number of new cases over a period divided by the people in the group, and in women with neither the mutation nor oral contraceptive use it is 0.8 per 10,000 women per year. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 17; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.5"},{"q":"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that occasionally can lead to pulmonary embolism and death. The incidence of DVT is increased by several genetic and environmental factors, including oral contraceptives (OCs) and a genetic mutation, factor V Leiden. These two risk factors, OCs and factor V Leiden, interact. Heterozygotes for factor V Leiden have 4 to 10 times the risk of DVT of the general population. In women without the genetic mutation, incidence of DVT rises from about 0.8/10,000 women/yr among those not on OCs to 3.0/10,000 women/yr for those taking the pill. The baseline incidence of DVT in heterozygotes for factor V Leiden is 5.7/10,000 women/yr, rising to 28.5/10,000 women/yr among those taking OCs. Mutations for factor V Leiden occur in about 5% of whites but are absent in Africans and Asians.\n\nWhat is the attributable risk of DVT for women taking OCs who do not carry the mutation for factor V Leiden compared to those not taking OCs and not carrying the mutation?","choices":["0.8/10,000/yr","1.3/10,000/yr","2.2/10,000/yr","9.5/10,000/yr","25.5/10,000/yr"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: attributable risk is the incidence in exposed persons minus the incidence in non-exposed persons, so among women without the mutation, oral contraceptive users (3.0) minus non-users (0.8) gives 2.2 extra cases per 10,000 women per year. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 18; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.6"},{"q":"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that occasionally can lead to pulmonary embolism and death. The incidence of DVT is increased by several genetic and environmental factors, including oral contraceptives (OCs) and a genetic mutation, factor V Leiden. These two risk factors, OCs and factor V Leiden, interact. Heterozygotes for factor V Leiden have 4 to 10 times the risk of DVT of the general population. In women without the genetic mutation, incidence of DVT rises from about 0.8/10,000 women/yr among those not on OCs to 3.0/10,000 women/yr for those taking the pill. The baseline incidence of DVT in heterozygotes for factor V Leiden is 5.7/10,000 women/yr, rising to 28.5/10,000 women/yr among those taking OCs. Mutations for factor V Leiden occur in about 5% of whites but are absent in Africans and Asians.\n\nWhat is the attributable risk of DVT for women taking OCs who carry factor V Leiden compared to women on OCs but not carrying the mutation?","choices":["0.8/10,000/yr","1.3/10,000/yr","2.2/10,000/yr","9.5/10,000/yr","25.5/10,000/yr"],"answer":4,"exp":"Correct: attributable risk is the incidence in exposed minus the incidence in non-exposed, and among oral contraceptive users the carriers (28.5) minus the non-carriers (3.0) gives 25.5 extra cases per 10,000 women per year attributable to the mutation. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 18; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.7"},{"q":"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that occasionally can lead to pulmonary embolism and death. The incidence of DVT is increased by several genetic and environmental factors, including oral contraceptives (OCs) and a genetic mutation, factor V Leiden. These two risk factors, OCs and factor V Leiden, interact. Heterozygotes for factor V Leiden have 4 to 10 times the risk of DVT of the general population. In women without the genetic mutation, incidence of DVT rises from about 0.8/10,000 women/yr among those not on OCs to 3.0/10,000 women/yr for those taking the pill. The baseline incidence of DVT in heterozygotes for factor V Leiden is 5.7/10,000 women/yr, rising to 28.5/10,000 women/yr among those taking OCs. Mutations for factor V Leiden occur in about 5% of whites but are absent in Africans and Asians.\n\nIn a population with 100,000 white women, all of whom take OCs, what is the population-attributable risk for DVT in women who are heterozygous for factor V Leiden?","choices":["0.8/10,000/yr","1.3/10,000/yr","2.2/10,000/yr","9.5/10,000/yr","25.5/10,000/yr"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct: population attributable risk is the attributable risk multiplied by the prevalence of the risk factor in the population, here 25.5 per 10,000 x 0.05 = 1.3 per 10,000 women per year, because only 5% of the women carry the mutation. — Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.8"},{"q":"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that occasionally can lead to pulmonary embolism and death. The incidence of DVT is increased by several genetic and environmental factors, including oral contraceptives (OCs) and a genetic mutation, factor V Leiden. These two risk factors, OCs and factor V Leiden, interact. Heterozygotes for factor V Leiden have 4 to 10 times the risk of DVT of the general population. In women without the genetic mutation, incidence of DVT rises from about 0.8/10,000 women/yr among those not on OCs to 3.0/10,000 women/yr for those taking the pill. The baseline incidence of DVT in heterozygotes for factor V Leiden is 5.7/10,000 women/yr, rising to 28.5/10,000 women/yr among those taking OCs. Mutations for factor V Leiden occur in about 5% of whites but are absent in Africans and Asians.\n\nWhat is the relative risk of DVT in women taking OCs and heterozygous for factor V Leiden compared to women who take OCs but do not carry the mutation?","choices":["3.8","7.1","9.5","28.5","35.6"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct: relative risk is the incidence in exposed divided by the incidence in non-exposed, and among oral contraceptive users the carriers (28.5) divided by the non-carriers (3.0) gives 9.5, so carriers are about 9.5 times as likely to have a clot. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.9"},{"q":"Deep venous thrombosis (DVT) is a serious condition that occasionally can lead to pulmonary embolism and death. The incidence of DVT is increased by several genetic and environmental factors, including oral contraceptives (OCs) and a genetic mutation, factor V Leiden. These two risk factors, OCs and factor V Leiden, interact. Heterozygotes for factor V Leiden have 4 to 10 times the risk of DVT of the general population. In women without the genetic mutation, incidence of DVT rises from about 0.8/10,000 women/yr among those not on OCs to 3.0/10,000 women/yr for those taking the pill. The baseline incidence of DVT in heterozygotes for factor V Leiden is 5.7/10,000 women/yr, rising to 28.5/10,000 women/yr among those taking OCs. Mutations for factor V Leiden occur in about 5% of whites but are absent in Africans and Asians.\n\nWhat is the relative risk of DVT in women taking OCs and without the mutation compared to women without the mutation who are not taking OCs?","choices":["3.8","7.1","9.5","28.5","35.6"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct: relative risk is the incidence in exposed divided by the incidence in non-exposed, and among women without the mutation the oral contraceptive users (3.0) divided by non-users (0.8) gives 3.75, about 3.8. — 7 PAJ5512 Risk MBF 2026sc.pptx, Slide 19; Clinical Epidemiology Study Questions, Question 6.10"}]};
